  "subtitle": "a 36-inch programmable floor robot that turns code into physical motion",
  "why_scout_cares": "The interaction model is radically simple: a child types code on an Apple II, and a life-sized humanoid robot across the room executes those commands. Topo has no sensors — it is a pure physical output device, a body for your program to inhabit. This is code made tangible, a direct ancestor of every educational robot from LEGO Mindstorms to Sphero. From Nolan Bushnell's post-Atari venture Androbot, launched May 1983 at $495. Only ~650 units shipped before the company folded. The robot has no autonomy, no sensing, no intelligence — and that is exactly what makes it HCI: the human programs, the robot performs, and the loop closes through observation of physical movement.",
